      "text": "Given that \\(AB\\) is the diameter of the circle centered at \\(O\\), and \\(E\\) is a point on the circle, we know that \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\) because the angle subtended by a diameter in a semicircle is a right angle.\n\nWe are given \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\). Since \\(AB\\) is the diameter, \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\). Therefore, \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\).\n\nNow, consider the tangent at \\(B\\) and the tangent at \\(E\\). The tangent at any point on a circle is perpendicular to the radius at that point. Therefore, the tangent at \\(B\\) is perpendicular to \\(OB\\) and the tangent at \\(E\\) is perpendicular to \\(OE\\).\n\nSince \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\), and \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\), we can find \\(\\angle ABE\\) as follows:\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\angle ABE\\) and \\(\\angle BAE\\) are vertically opposite angles (formed by the two intersecting lines \\(BE\\) and \\(AE\\)).\n\nNext, we need to find \\(\\angle CED\\). Note that \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are points where the tangent at \\(B\\) intersects \\(AE\\) and \\(AB\\), respectively. The line \\(CD\\) is the polar of \\(E\\) with respect to the circle. The angle between the tangents drawn from an external point to a circle is twice the angle subtended by the chord joining the points of tangency.\n\nSince \\(OB\\) and \\(OE\\) are radii and \\(AB\\) is the diameter, \\(OB = OE = R\\) where \\(R\\) is the radius of the circle. The angle \\(\\angle BOE\\) is a central angle and is twice the angle subtended by the chord \\(BE\\). Since \\(E\\) lies on the circle, \\(\\angle BOE = 2 \\times 43^\\circ = 86^\\circ\\) (angle \\(BOE\\) is an arc angle, and \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\) is half of it).\n\nThe angle \\(\\angle CED\\) is the angle between the tangents at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) which subtends the arc \\(BE\\). The angle between the tangents is twice the angle subtended by the arc \\(BE\\), so:\n\\[\n\\angle CED = 2 \\times \\angle BOE = 2 \\times 86^\\circ = 172^\\circ\n\\]\nHowever, we need to simplify this angle in the context of the circle, where the angle is measured in the plane of the circle. The angle between the tangents at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) is actually the angle between the tangents, which is equivalent to half the central angle subtended by the arc \\(BE\\), so:\n\\[\n\\angle CED = 180^\\circ - 2 \\times 43^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 86^\\circ = 94^\\circ\n\\]\nThus, the correct angle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{94}\n\\]",
