      "text": "This problem is essentially testing our understanding of basic geometry concepts, specifically the tangent-secant angle theorem and the angles in a circle.\n\nFirst, note that the tangent-secant angle theorem states that the angle between a tangent and a chord through the point of tangency is equal to half the measure of the intercepted arc (the arc created by the chord). In this case, triangles $\\Delta ABE$ and $\\Delta AOE$ share a common point $A$, so we can use the fact that the angles in a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$.\n\nSince $\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ$ and both $\\angle ABE$ and $\\angle AOE$ are right angles (as they are tangents), we know that $\\angle BAE = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\angle ABE + \\angle AEO \\right)$. Thus,\n\\[\n43^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\angle ABE + \\angle AEO \\right).\n\\]\nSince $\\angle ABE = \\angle AEO = 90^\\circ$, we have\n\\[\n43^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( 90^\\circ + 90^\\circ \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 180^\\circ = 90^\\circ.\n\\]\nThus, $\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ$ is proven.\n\nNext, consider $\\Delta BCD$. This triangle is isosceles because $BC = BD$ (as they are both radii of the circle). Therefore, $\\angle BCD = \\angle CBD$. Let $\\angle BCD = \\angle CBD = x$. Then, by the angle sum property of triangles,\n\\[\n\\angle BCD + \\angle CBD + \\angle CDB = 180^\\circ,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nx + x + \\angle CDB = 180^\\circ,\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to\n\\[\n2x + \\angle CDB = 180^\\circ.\n\\]\nSince $\\angle CDB$ is the external angle of $\\Delta ABC$ at $B$, we have $\\angle CDB = 180^\\circ - \\angle ABC$. However, notice that $\\angle ABC$ is an inscribed angle subtended by the arc $AE$, so $\\angle ABC = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\angle A + \\angle AEC \\right)$. But we know that $AE = AE$, so $\\angle AEC = x$ (since it's the same angle as $\\angle BCD$).\n\nThus, we have\n\\[\n\\angle ABC = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( 90^\\circ + x \\right).\n\\]\nSince $\\angle CDB = 180^\\circ - \\left( 90^\\circ + x \\right) = 90^\\circ - x$, we have\n\\[\n2x + (90^\\circ - x) = 180^\\circ,\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to\n\\[\nx + 90^\\circ = 180^\\circ,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nx = 90^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 47^\\circ.\n\\]\nTherefore, $\\angle BCD = \\angle CBD = 47^\\circ$. Now, looking at $\\Delta CDE$, we have $\\angle CED = 180^\\circ - \\angle CDE - \\angle DEC$. Since $\\angle CDE = 90^\\circ - 47^\\circ = 43^\\circ$ and $\\angle DEC = 47^\\circ$, we have\n\\[\n\\angle CED = 180^\\circ - 47^\\circ - 43^\\circ = 90^\\circ - 43^\\circ = 47^\\circ.\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{47}\n\\]",
